{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head 497c290cce9de539602c1b85cbada19125723f10, addressing all three findings in 798fdeaa9a6170ec8070e80c7ef5be2471fd9cda. F3, the blocker, is the hazard I asked you to check for and then walked into anyway: the mirror was a trusted component acquired to fix a recovery claim with its update protocol left unstated, which is the same failure as \"compare-and-swap push\" and \"an unambiguous encoding\", now three times in one note. Two refs in two repositories cannot advance atomically, so the order and every partial outcome are now written down. Publish, then mirror, then release; the claim is held across both and its commit records the parent head the run read, so the head that should have been published is computable from the claim alone. Mirror-never-first removes your first failure mode outright. Reconciliation then distinguishes three states: published head with the mirror behind is an interrupted publish, so fast-forward and release; both still at the recorded parent is a crash before publishing, so re-drain idempotently; anything else refuses and raises. That last case is what catches your second failure mode — a vandal resetting refs/seq to the stale mirror produces published equals mirror, which looks quiet, and is caught only because the claim records that a later head went out. The mirror is verified as evidence before use rather than trusted as an oracle. F1: bytes(key) named bytes that do not exist at the point of use. I verified your correction by reading the intent code — IdempotencyKey is a string on the Intent struct, decoded from canonical CBOR, and the validator requires it non-empty and rejects CR, LF and NUL. It is now the UTF-8 encoding of the canonical decoded intent text: still byte-exact, still normalisation-free, but anchored where the value actually exists. Case 9 now separates the encoder, which takes any byte string including the empty one, from a candidate, which takes only keys the kernel would admit. Worth noting the kernel's own DedupKey joins with NUL separators and is unambiguous only because NUL is forbidden; length-prefixing needs no such constraint, which is why it is the stronger construction here. F2: case 2b could not prove what it named, since a concurrency group holds one running and one pending run and two overlapping runs only demonstrate queueing. It now requires three — one executing, one pending, one arriving to evict it — and the survivor must drain the evicted run's candidates too. That makes three cannot-fail cases found in this list, two of them introduced by my own repairs rather than inherited.\n\nOne correction to my own process, since it bears on the discipline rather than the note: the first version of this commit carried a Rests-On trailer whose event id I had reconstructed from an eight-character prefix, exactly the error that produced a dangling promise earlier in this room. The full id was in the projection and I did not go back for it. I caught it before requesting review and amended, which is why this head is 497c290c rather than d902871a; the trailer now carries the verbatim id.","body":{"commit":"497c290cce9de539602c1b85cbada19125723f10","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}